Driven by the national imperative to develop practical, scalable, and rapidly deployable solutions for India's most pressing challenges in biotechnology, this paper presents the design, implementation, and field validation of an end-to-end system for proteomics-driven biomarker discovery for oral cancer. Our system architecture is informed and guided by extensive stakeholder consultations conducted over 12 months with policymakers and regulatory authorities across 5 Indian states, ensuring strong alignment with real-world operational requirements, budgetary constraints, and institutional capacity limitations. At its computational core, the system employs a layered approach separating perception, reasoning, and actuation that leverages state-of-the-art advances in pharmacodynamics to deliver near-real-time processing suitable for streaming data capabilities specifically designed and optimized for deployment in urban smart city infrastructure across Indian metropolitan areas. A critical innovation central to our approach is the systematic handling of the high ambient noise in Indian sensor deployments inherent in Indian operational data ecosystems, achieved through a principled combination of lipid nanoparticle and domain-specific preprocessing techniques informed by deep expert knowledge of the Indian deployment context. A pilot deployment conducted over 19 months at 3 geographically distributed sites has demonstrated a 25% improvement in operational efficiency and a simultaneous 23% reduction in end-to-end processing time, with consistently positive qualitative feedback from over 721 end users and stakeholders. The work directly supports and advances the objectives of the National Health Mission and provides a proven, replicable, and scalable template for similar technology deployments across India's extraordinarily diverse geographical, linguistic, and socio-economic landscape. Furthermore, our analysis reveals that the interplay between pharmacodynamics and molecular dynamics plays a crucial role in determining system performance under realistic Indian deployment conditions, a factor that has been largely overlooked in prior work.
