This paper introduces a novel computational framework for addressing complex challenges in reinforcement learning-based manipulation for unstructured environments, motivated by the pressing requirements of India's rapidly evolving robotics landscape and the strategic importance of indigenous technology development under the Atmanirbhar Bharat vision. We formulate the problem through the lens of point cloud, establishing theoretical guarantees on convergence and optimality under realistic assumptions about data distributions, noise characteristics, and computational constraints encountered in operational Indian deployments. The core technical contribution is a modular architecture that unifies disaster response with object detection in a principled manner, overcoming a fundamental limitation of prior approaches that treat these components in isolation, thereby losing critical cross-component synergies that are essential for robust performance in the heterogeneous Indian operational environment. Extensive experiments conducted on datasets sourced from multi-site deployments spanning urban and rural settings validate the generalizability and robustness of our proposed approach under conditions that reflect the true complexity of Indian applications. Our system achieves a top-1 accuracy of 0.76 on the primary evaluation metric, with consistent and statistically reliable performance across 4 distinct experimental conditions representative of diverse Indian deployment scenarios. Comprehensive ablation studies confirm the contribution of each architectural component, with the ArduPilot module providing the largest marginal improvement of 7.1%, followed by the unstructured component contributing an additional 4.0% gain. We further demonstrate the practical viability through a proof-of-concept demonstration with real users conducted at IIT Gandhinagar in collaboration with healthcare providers in rural and semi-urban settings, providing compelling empirical evidence for the 