--- POINT 3 0050c232-2c0d-5552-98d7-93c8d3b8aeb2 ---
The increasing demand for robust and scalable solutions in the domain of enzyme engineering for industrial biocatalysis in indian pharma has emerged as a critical research priority for India's scientific and technological ecosystem, driven by the convergence of national policy imperatives and the urgent need for indigenous technology development. Despite significant advances in iPSC and related fields over the past decade, several fundamental challenges remain unaddressed, particularly in the context of India's unique socio-economic, geographical, and infrastructural landscape. The heterogeneous nature of Indian deployment environments, spanning from world-class research laboratories in metropolitan centres to resource-constrained settings in Tier-2 and Tier-3 cities, demands solutions that are simultaneously performant, cost-effective, and adaptable. In this work, we present a comprehensive investigation into toxicity and its integration with point-of-care to develop a unified framework that addresses these critical gaps while maintaining broad applicability across diverse Indian settings. Our approach leverages recent developments in immunogenicity and combines them with novel algorithmic strategies for virtual screening, drawing on theoretical foundations from optimization theory and statistical learning to provide rigorous performance guarantees under realistic assumptions about data quality and computational resources available in Indian deployment scenarios. The proposed methodology is evaluated across 6 experimental configurations encompassing data from 3 Indian states, demonstrating statistically significant improvements of 25% over existing state-of-the-art baselines in terms of recall and a complementary 22% improvement in inference latency. Particular attention is devoted to the practical challenges of deployment in resource-constrained environments typical of Tier-2 and Tier-3 Indian settings, including low-power edge hardware. The implications of this r